==Portuguese==
[[Image:Alopekis puppies.jpg|thumb|250px|cachorros (sense 1)]]
[[Image:Bernese Dog 10m..jpg|thumb|250px|cachorro (sense 2)]]

===Etymology===
{{unc|pt}},<ref>{{R:pt:Infopédia}}</ref><ref>{{R:pt:Priberam}}</ref> perhaps from {{der|pt|eu|txakur}}, with metathesis and possibly via {{der|pt|es|cachorro||cub}}. Compare also {{cog|it|cucciolo}} and {{cog|co|ghjacaru}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{pt-IPA|cachôrro}}
* {{a|Northern Portugal}} {{IPA|pt|[ˈkɐ.t͡ʃo.ʁu]}}
* {{a|Rural Central Brazil}} {{IPA|pt|/kɐˈʃo.hʷ/}}
* {{hyphenation|pt|ca|chor|ro}}

===Noun===
{{pt-noun|m|f=+}}

# {{lb|pt|Portugal}} [[puppy]] {{gloss|a young dog}}
# {{lb|pt|Brazil|Madeira|colloquial}} [[dog]] (of any age)
# {{lb|pt|Brazil|derogatory}} a [[promiscuous]] man
# {{lb|pt|Brazil|by extension}} an unfaithful man
# {{ellipsis of|pt|cachorro-quente|dot=:}} [[hot dog]]

====Usage notes====
In Brazil, this is the neutral and colloquial term for "dog", whereas in settings of higher formalities {{m|pt|cão}} is used instead. The feminine {{m|pt|cachorra}} is even more avoided in such settings (in which {{m|pt|cadela}} is used), but is used more often in everyday language.

====Synonyms====
